Title: Adopt RFC 2119 normative keywords across the specification chain
Created: 2026-08-29
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-08-29T00:00Z claude created

2026-08-29T13:47Z Minh Ha-Duong note blocker 0036 closed — Blocked-by removed.
--- body ---
## Context
Concatenated, the four specification-chain documents run 1 356 lines and
11 862 words — RFC-sized. They carry zero uses of the uppercase RFC 2119
convention (MUST, SHOULD, MAY, SHALL, REQUIRED, RECOMMENDED). Modal verbs
do appear, on 24 lines, all lowercase: R26 says "Coverage must reach 100 %"
in the same typography the surrounding narrative uses for emphasis.

The cost is that an implementer cannot tell which lines are load-bearing.
REQUIREMENTS.md calls each R-item "a testable property", but a property
stated as plain assertion does not say whether it may be traded away under
pressure. RFC 2119 exists for this: it separates the contract from the
commentary, visibly to a reader and greppably to a script.

This is a typography and phrasing pass over existing meaning. It changes no
requirement, threshold or budget. Anything that reads like a new ruling
goes through DECISIONS.md first, not here — the bound 0036 works under.

Blocked by 0036: that ticket's last open criterion is the author's
acceptance of the rewritten voice. Applying a keyword convention to prose
that may still be vetoed would be redone.

## Relevant files
- `spec/REQUIREMENTS.md` — R1–R28, the main site
- `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md` — C1–C4; constraints are facts about the world, so most
  should NOT take normative keywords; the design's obligations under them
  may
- `spec/DESIGN.md` — §2 architecture, §2.8 gates, §2.9 budgets
- `spec/DECISIONS.md` — out of scope, as in 0036: an append-only ledger, not
  prose to restyle

## Actions
1. Add a short "Normative language" paragraph to REQUIREMENTS.md's Intro,
   citing RFC 2119 and stating that the uppercase keywords carry contract
   force and lowercase modal verbs do not.
2. Pass over each R-item and decide its force. Most R-items are MUST. Some
   are genuinely SHOULD (a preference with a stated escape) — say which,
   and why, rather than inflating everything to MUST.
3. Pass over DESIGN.md §2.8 gates and §2.9 budgets: a gate threshold is a
   MUST, a budget is likelier a SHOULD with a measured escape.
4. Leave CONSTRAINTS.md's statements of fact alone. A constraint is not an
   obligation; only the design's response to it can be.

## Test
An adherence-style check that greps the spec chain for lowercase modal
verbs inside R-item and gate lines and reports them, so the convention
cannot silently erode. Red first: it must fire on the current text.

## Verification
- [ ] Every R-item's force is explicit and deliberate, not defaulted
- [ ] No requirement changed in substance (diff reviewed against
      DECISIONS.md)
- [ ] `make check` green; figure-guard anchors follow any reworded sentence

## Invariants
- Meaning is preserved. This is a rewording.
- Numbers keep decimal comma and space thousands.
- R-, C- and D-numbers and section numbers survive: tickets and
  DECISIONS.md point into them.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The spec chain uses the uppercase convention, declared once in
      REQUIREMENTS.md's Intro
- [ ] Constraints stay descriptive; only obligations take keywords
- [ ] A grep-level guard keeps the convention from eroding
- [ ] `make check` passes
